.TH GETDELAYS 8 2026-09-21 "Linux 7.0.9" "System Manager's Manual"
.SH NAME
getdelays \- print per-task and per-process delay accounting statistics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B getdelays
.RB [ \-dilqv ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR logfile ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR bufsize ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR cpumask ]
.RB [ \-p
.I pid
|
.B \-t
.I tgid
|
.B \-C
.IR cgroup-dir ]
.br
.B getdelays
.RB [ \-dilqv ]
.RB [ \-w
.IR logfile ]
.RB [ \-r
.IR bufsize ]
.RB [ \-m
.IR cpumask ]
.B \-c
.I command
.RI [ argument ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B getdelays
requests statistics from the kernel's taskstats interface, a generic
netlink family, and prints them.
It is mainly used to show delay accounting data: the time a task has
spent waiting for a CPU, for synchronous block I/O, for swap-in, for
memory reclaim, for thrashing, for memory compaction, for write-protect
copies and for IRQ handling.
The Linux source describes it as a simple utility that also illustrates
the taskstats interface.
.PP
.B getdelays
works in one or more of these modes:
.IP \(bu 3
With
.B \-p
or
.BR \-t ,
it requests the statistics of one task or of a whole thread group
(process).
.IP \(bu 3
With
.BR \-c ,
it runs
.IR command ,
waits for it to exit, and then requests the statistics of the
command's process by its process ID.
.IP \(bu 3
With
.BR \-m ,
it registers to receive the statistics that the kernel sends when a task
exits on one of the CPUs in
.IR cpumask .
.IP \(bu 3
With
.BR \-C ,
it requests the number of tasks in each scheduling state in a cgroup.
.PP
At least one of
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-t ,
.BR \-c ,
.B \-m
and
.B \-C
must be given; otherwise a usage message is printed.
.B \-C
cannot be combined with
.BR \-p ,
.B \-t
or
.BR \-c .
If more than one of
.BR \-p ,
.B \-t
and
.B \-c
is given, the last one takes effect.
.PP
Unless
.B \-l
is given,
.B getdelays
exits after it has processed the first statistics record it receives.
What it prints for a record is selected by
.BR \-d ,
.B \-i
and
.BR \-q ;
without any of them, task statistics are received but not printed.
Cgroup statistics requested with
.B \-C
are always printed, as a line of the form
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
sleeping N, blocked N, running N, stopped N, uninterruptible N
.EE
.in
.PP
.B getdelays
also prints a line to standard output for most options it processes,
such as
.RB \(dq "print delayacct stats ON" \(dq.
.SS Delay accounting output
With
.BR \-d ,
each record is introduced by a line
.BI PID " pid"
or
.BI TGID " tgid" \fR,\fP
followed by a table for each delay type:
.BR CPU ,
.BR IO ,
.BR SWAP ,
.BR RECLAIM ,
.BR THRASHING ,
.BR COMPACT ,
.B WPCOPY
and
.BR IRQ .
For each type the table shows the number of delays recorded
.RB ( count ),
their cumulative total in nanoseconds
.RB ( "delay total" ),
and the average, maximum and minimum delay in milliseconds.
The last column is the local time, in the form
.IR YYYY\-MM\-DD T hh:mm:ss ,
at which the maximum delay occurred, or
.B N/A
if none was recorded.
The
.B CPU
table also shows the task's cumulative running time in nanoseconds, both
as wall-clock time
.RB ( "real total" )
and as seen by the kernel without adjustment for time stolen by
virtualization
.RB ( "virtual total" ).
.PP
The columns and tables printed depend on the version of the taskstats
structure reported by the kernel: the maximum and minimum columns need
version 16, the timestamp column version 17, and the COMPACT, WPCOPY and
IRQ tables versions 11, 13 and 14 respectively.
The Linux 7.0.9 kernel reports version 17.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " pid"
Request the statistics of the task with thread ID
.IR pid .
.TP
.BI \-t " tgid"
Request the statistics of the thread group
.IR tgid ,
summed over its threads; the delay statistics also include threads that
have exited.
.TP
.BI \-c " command " \fR[\fIargument\fR...]
Run
.I command
with the given arguments, searching
.B PATH
for it, and when it exits request the statistics of its process by
process ID (as with
.BR \-p ).
.I command
must be a separate argument, not attached to
.BR \-c .
Option processing stops at
.BR \-c ;
all remaining arguments are passed to
.IR command .
.TP
.BI \-m " cpumask"
Register to receive the statistics of tasks that exit on the CPUs in
.IR cpumask ,
a comma-separated list of CPU numbers and ranges such as
.BR 1\-3,5,7\-8 .
When the last thread of a process exits, the kernel also sends the
statistics of the thread group.
When
.B getdelays
leaves its receive loop it removes the registration; if it is killed
instead, for example while running with
.BR \-l ,
the kernel removes the registration later.
.TP
.BI \-C " cgroup-dir"
Print the number of tasks in the cgroup directory
.I cgroup-dir
that are sleeping, blocked on I/O, running, stopped and in uninterruptible
sleep.
.TP
.B \-d
Print delay accounting statistics, as described under
.BR "Delay accounting output" .
.TP
.B \-i
Print I/O accounting statistics as
.IB command ": read=" N ", write=" N ", \%cancelled_write=" N\fR,\fP
where the values are byte counts.
The kernel fills these fields only in per-task statistics, so they are
zero in thread group statistics, which are requested with
.B \-t
and also sent with
.BR \-m .
.TP
.B \-q
Print the task's voluntary and involuntary context switch counts.
.TP
.B \-l
Keep receiving and printing statistics records until interrupted,
instead of exiting after the first one.
This is normally used with
.BR \-m .
.TP
.BI \-w " logfile"
Write each task statistics record received, as raw binary data, to
.IR logfile .
The file is created if necessary and truncated.
.TP
.BI \-r " bufsize"
Set the receive buffer size of the netlink socket to
.I bufsize
bytes.
.TP
.B \-v
Print debugging messages about the netlink exchange.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The exit status does not reliably indicate success.
.B getdelays
exits with status 0 after printing statistics, but also after printing
the usage message because no request was given and after most errors,
including errors returned by the kernel.
It exits with status 1 if the argument of
.B \-p
or
.B \-t
is zero or not a number, if the argument of
.B \-r
is negative, if the netlink socket cannot be created, if
.I logfile
cannot be opened or written, or if the child process for
.B \-c
cannot be created.
It exits with status 255 if an unknown option is given or if the
registration made with
.B \-m
cannot be removed.
.SH NOTES
The kernel must be built with
.B \%CONFIG_TASKSTATS
and, for delay statistics,
.BR \%CONFIG_TASK_DELAY_ACCT .
Delay accounting is disabled by default; it is enabled with the
.B delayacct
kernel command-line parameter or at run time with the
.B kernel.task_delayacct
sysctl, and in the latter case only tasks started after it was enabled
have delay data.
The I/O statistics printed by
.B \-i
need
.B \%CONFIG_TASK_XACCT
and
.BR \%CONFIG_TASK_IO_ACCOUNTING .
.PP
The kernel accepts the taskstats request used by
.BR \-p ,
.BR \-t ,
.B \-c
and
.B \-m
only from a caller with the
.B \%CAP_NET_ADMIN
capability (on Peios,
.BR \%SeTcbPrivilege );
otherwise
.B getdelays
reports a fatal reply error.
The cgroup request used by
.B \-C
needs no capability, but
.I cgroup-dir
must be a directory of a version 1 cgroup hierarchy (a file system of
type
.BR cgroup );
the kernel rejects any other directory.
.PP
With
.BR \-c ,
the statistics are those of the command's initial thread only, because
they are requested by process ID rather than thread group ID.
.PP
The taskstats structure, the taskstats interface and delay accounting
are described in these files in the Linux source:
.RS
.I include/uapi/linux/taskstats.h
.br
.I Documentation/accounting/taskstats.rst
.br
.I Documentation/accounting/delay\-accounting.rst
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
Print the delay statistics of the thread group 242:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
getdelays \-d \-t 242
.EE
.in
.PP
Run a command and print its delay and I/O statistics:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
getdelays \-d \-i \-c make \-j4
.EE
.in
.PP
Print the delay statistics of every task that exits on CPUs 0 to 3:
.PP
.in +4n
.EX
getdelays \-d \-l \-m 0\-3
.EE
.in
